     <?MarkedContent page="1" ?> 
     <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>Intro
    </section-heading>
    <semantic-para xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
       rolemaps-to="Part"
      >
     <text-block xmlns="https://www.latex-project.org/ns/dflt"
        xmlns:Layout="http://iso.org/pdf/ssn/Layout"
        Layout:TextAlign="Justify"
        rolemaps-to="P"
       >
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>In the early nineties, 
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>GSM
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?> was deployed in many European countries. Global System for Mobile communication (
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>GSM
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>) offered for the first time international roaming for mobile subscribers. The 
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>GSM
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>’s use of Time Division Multiple Access (
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>TDMA
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>) as its communication standard was debated at length. And every now and then there are big discussion whether Code Division Multiple Access (
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>CDMA
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>) should have been chosen over 
      <Link xmlns="http://iso.org/pdf2/ssn"
        >
       <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>TDMA
       <?ReferencedObject type="Annot" page="1" ?>
      </Link>
      <?MarkedContent page="1" ?>.
     </text-block>
    </semantic-para>
   </section>
